courier (1.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Used as a library, courier reads only what it is handed:
    CourierConfig.from_dict reads only its dict, and ImapClient, search
    and watch apply a WORLD_AS_OF bound only when it is passed as
    world_as_of=, an aware datetime. The courier command and the MCP
    server read the config file, .env and WORLD_AS_OF and pass them in;
    load_config() is the command's loader, and fills the credentials a
    config file leaves out from IMAP_PASSWORD and GMAIL_*.
  * Importing courier has no side effect: it loads no .env file and
    configures no logging.
  * courier.search(config, query, blocks=...) runs one query over
    [imap.NAME] blocks and gives each block's result a status: complete,
    partial (folders_failed names the folders that failed) or failed
    (error gives the reason, such as connection failed: <reason>). The
    search command's JSON carries the same status per block.
  * ImapClient.fetch_by_message_id(message_id) returns the one message a
    Message-ID names, or None when every folder asked was searched and
    none holds it; copies in several folders raise AmbiguousMessageId,
    whose matches lists each (folder, uid), and a folder whose search
    failed raises TransientError.
  * Email.to_dict() gives the object courier read prints for the
    message. read --message-id reports uid null for a message it serves
    from a maildir file with no UID.
  * ImapClient is a context manager: it connects when a call first needs
    the server and disconnects when the with block ends.
  * WorldAsOfInvalid (a malformed or naive bound) and WorldBoundRefused
    (an operation that would surface data dated after the bound) are
    exported from courier.
  * The MCP server run on its own (python -m courier.mcp_server) refuses
    a malformed WORLD_AS_OF with one line, Error: <reason>, and exit
    status 1.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

courier (1.1.22) unstable; urgency=medium

  * An IMAP connection opens only when a call needs the server: a search
    or read the local index answers makes none, and in:sent is served
    from the index when the Sent folder is synced.
  * read --message-id resolves the message through the local index
    first, falling back to a live search.
  * An explicit --cc on a reply-all adds to the thread's Cc instead of
    replacing it; Cc is deduplicated case-insensitively. Closes #70.
  * Every run keeps the Claude Code command file current;
    install-claude-command writes it unconditionally and no longer takes
    --yes.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

